<p>We haven’t gone away on holiday for the past couple of years. It doesn’t feel fair to leave Bianca (now a grand old lady of 20) in a boarding cattery, so either only one of us can go away (the other staying at home to look after Bianca), or neither of us do. Mr. Bsag very kindly volunteered to do the cat-sitting for a couple of successive years, but it didn’t feel the same going away without him. Now that my ME/CFS is worse, it has also taken away the main thing that we went away for: taking long walks in the countryside.</p><p>I have still taken time off work of course (at the time of writing, I’m nearing the end of a period of annual leave), and that provides lots of opportunities to potter and engage in my many hobbies, so it’s not all bad. We also try to schedule in a day trip to somewhere that’s quiet and in the countryside. On this occasion it was <a href="https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/visit/gloucestershire-cotswolds/hidcote">Hidcote</a>, a garden managed by the <a href="https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/">National Trust</a>. It was one of the nicest ‘days out’ we’ve had in a while.</p><p>The trip was preceeded by a great deal of planning. Looking at the weather forecast, we could see that another heatwave was approaching. The heat exacerbates my symptoms pretty badly, so we wanted to visit while the temperatures were still relatively bearable. We’ve been to Hidcote before, and know that it can be busy in the summer. It’s a big garden, so you can still find quiet spots, but we still wanted to go at the quietest time we could. In the end, we picked Monday (of this week) and kept our fingers crossed that everything would align.</p><p>It was a perfect day. It was sunny, but still a bearable temperature (in the low 20°C-24°C range), and a gentle breeze keeping things fresh. It also turned out to be quite a quiet day visitor-wise, and though it certainly wasn’t empty, people weren’t piling up in the popular spots (like the famous Red Borders and Gazebos), nor was the café too busy when we needed it.</p><p>We wandered gently around, feeling like it was a dream world. At home, we are constantly assailed by the noise of cars, trucks servicing a branch of Tescos, and people shouting at each other for seemingly no reason, at all hours of the day and night. It’s exhausting. Hidcote wasn’t devoid of people, but we were far away from cars and traffic. All we could see was a lovely house, trees and flowers, and all we could hear were natural sounds and the quiet conversation of people.</p><figure><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-400.webp 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-800.webp 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-2250.webp 2250w" sizes="auto"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-400.jpeg" alt="Looking up through maple leaves at a deep blue sky. The leaves glow orange-red in the light." width="2250" height="3000" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-400.jpeg 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-800.jpeg 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/Kx717u0PTH-2250.jpeg 2250w" sizes="auto"></picture><figcaption>Maple trees bordering the Maple Garden</figcaption></figure><p>The gardens have suffered (as they had on our last visit) from the dry summer we have had, so all the lawns were brown and some of the plants were clearly struggling. However, there was still plenty to see and some astonishing colours. We also saw lots of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hummingbird_hawk-moth">hummingbird hawk-moths</a> feeding on the flowers. I think I saw my first ever hummingbird hawk-moth there years ago, and now here were dozens of them, busily feeding from flowers. I love to see them, because their pattern of flight is so like a hummingbird’s that it makes you feel as if you are somewhere quite tropical.</p><figure><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-400.webp 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-800.webp 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-3000.webp 3000w" sizes="auto"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-400.jpeg" alt="Three overlapping trees of different heights fill most of the frame: a dark green cedar tree, a maple with deep red leaves, and another with yellow-green leaves. To the left, a clipped yew hedge borders the garden, and the beds are filled with plants that have silver or purple leaves and flowers." width="3000" height="2250" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-400.jpeg 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-800.jpeg 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/OlGiF-Fvv5-3000.jpeg 3000w" sizes="auto"></picture><figcaption>A view across the Maple Garden</figcaption></figure><p>The garden is — of course — beautifully designed, so there are viewpoints to catch your eye, or combinations of colours that jump out at you. The Maple Garden had low beds of silver and purple plants, contrasting with the fiery, rusty reds of the maples surrounding it. The Red Borders obviously feature red prominently, but also deep oranges and purples that all clash together in a delightful way. In the bright sunlight, it felt positively psychedelic, and — as a colour lover — I feasted my eyes on it.</p><figure><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-400.webp 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-800.webp 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-2250.webp 2250w" sizes="auto"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-400.jpeg" alt="Close up of a flower with deep red, velvety petals and a deep yellow centre. Below it is the leaf of a castor oil plant which has a bright red stem and leaf veins and the leaf looks like gold in the sunlight." width="2250" height="3000" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-400.jpeg 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-800.jpeg 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/0eYJ3JZUCs-2250.jpeg 2250w" sizes="auto"></picture><figcaption>Stunning deep red and orange-red flowers and foliage in the Red Border</figcaption></figure><p>There are also plenty of places to rest your eyes on shades of green. The Hazel Walk (where hazel plants are grown over a metal frame to form a tunnel), opens out looking over a terraced part of the garden. If I ever live in a place with a large enough garden, I’m definitely going to plant a hazel walk (even a small one), as they are deliciously cool and shady on a hot day. The breeze still passes through the leaves, but you get that perfect, cool, humidity inside the tunnel. Little spots of sunlight hit the path like starlight. The floor of the walk was a gravel path, and I didn’t want to hog the only seat at the end of it, so I ended up resting somewhere else.</p><figure><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-400.webp 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-800.webp 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-2250.webp 2250w" sizes="auto"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-400.jpeg" alt="You are looking down a tunnel composed of hazel plants grown over a frame of u-shaped metal supports, so that it is completely enclosed. There are spots of sunlight on the path in the shade, and at the end of the tunnel, a chair is placed so that visitors can look out at the view over another garden." width="2250" height="3000" srcset="https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-400.jpeg 400w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-800.jpeg 800w, https://www.rousette.org.uk/images/content/vnSz8xQxpt-2250.jpeg 2250w" sizes="auto"></picture><figcaption>Looking down the Hazel Walk towards the viewpoint at the end</figcaption></figure><p>The Long Walk (which you can see in 3D on <a href="https://3d.hypamaps.com/national-trust-hidcote">this map</a>) is a long, straight piece of lawn (or, currently, very brown grass), which is bordered by tall beech hedges. At the end, there’s a view out to the surrounding countryside, or you can look back through the Gazebos in the other direction. In the early afternoon, when I was beginning to tire, I found a spot in the shade of one of the hedges, and lay down to rest, while Mr. Bsag got out his sketchbook. It was such a peaceful spot. Every time I opened my eyes, all I could see was the rich green leaves of the hedge, the branches of a cedar tree and the blue sky.</p><p>Feeling restored, we spent the rest of the afternoon wandering, before reluctantly returning to our car. The car park was nearly empty when we left, and as we walked down it, a rabbit hurtled towards us, seemingly only seeing us at the last minute and veering off into the woods that border the car park. It was only one day, but it was a wonderful escape.</p>